</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before"> and </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Luis Jorge Sánchez Saldaña
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_address"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Address: </span>Departamento de Matemáticas, Facultad de Ciencias, Universidad Nacional Autónoma de México
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email address: </span><a href="mailto:luisjorge@ciencias.unam.mx">luisjorge@ciencias.unam.mx</a>
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract.</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">We show that the <span id="abstract1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full</span> mapping class group of any orientable closed surface with punctures admits a cocompact classifying space for proper actions of dimension equal to its virtual cohomological dimension. This was proved for closed orientable surfaces and for <span id="abstract1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pure</span> mapping class groups by Aramayona and Martínez Pérez. As a consequence of our result we also obtain the proper geometric dimension of <span id="abstract1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full</span> spherical braid groups.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_keywords">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_keywords">Key words and phrases: </h6>Mapping class groups, proper geometric dimension, classifying space for proper actions
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">1991 Mathematics Subject Classification</h6>57K20, 20J05, 20F65.
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1. </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> be an orientable closed connected surface of genus <math id="S1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and for <math id="S1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> consider <math id="S1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> a collection of distinguished points in <math id="S1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math>. The <span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">mapping class group</span> <math id="S1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is the group of isotopy classes of all orientation-preserving homeomorphisms <math id="S1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f:S_{g}^{n}\rightarrow S_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f:S_{g}^{n}\rightarrow S_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, where <math id="S1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}:=S_{g}-\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}:=S_{g}-\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. The group <math id="S1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> permutes the set <math id="S1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{p_{1},\ldots,p_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and the kernel of such action is the <span id="S1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pure mapping class group</span> <math id="S1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PMod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>PMod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PMod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we remove it from the notation.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we compute the minimal dimension <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, often called the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">proper geometric dimension</em>,
of a classifying space <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for proper actions of <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Recall that, for a discrete group <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, the space <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math> is a contractible <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-CW-complex on which G acts properly, and such that the fixed point set of a subgroup <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H&lt;G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H&lt;G</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible if <math id="S1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, and is empty
otherwise.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually torsion-free, its virtual cohomological dimension <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>
is a lower bound for <math id="S1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. Our main result is the following:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, the proper geometric dimension of <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is</span></p>
<table id="S1.Ex1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Moreover, there exists a cocompact <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension equal to <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The computation of the proper geometric dimension follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> for <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> for <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S5.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 5.1. ‣ 5. Genus 2 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5.1</span></a> for <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 6.2. ‣ 6. Genus at least 3 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> for <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. These theorems are the main results of Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S4" title="4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, <a href="#S5" title="5. Genus 2 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, and <a href="#S6" title="6. Genus at least 3 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> respectively, which provides a fair overview of the structure of the paper. For <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2g+n&gt;2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2g+n&gt;2</annotation></semantics></math>, the Teichmüller space <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is known to be a model for <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>; see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">JW10</a>, Proposition 2.3]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc05</a>, Section 4.10]</cite>. In fact,
Ji and Wolpert proved in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">JW10</a>, Theorem 1.2]</cite> that the <span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">truncated</span> Teichmüller space <math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}(\epsilon)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>ϵ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}(\epsilon)</annotation></semantics></math> is a cocompact classifying space for proper actions for <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\epsilon" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϵ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\epsilon</annotation></semantics></math> sufficiently small. Hence, the <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moreover</span> part of the main result follows from our computation of <math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> and a result of Lück <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc89</a>, Theorem 13.19]</cite> (see <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 2.1. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> below).</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1. </span>Known results and filling a gap in the literature</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>, Section 2]</cite> Harer constructed a <span id="S1.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">spine</span> of Teichmüller space, that is a <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PMod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>PMod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PMod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>-equivariant deformation retraction of <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi class="ltx_font_mathscript">𝒯</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathscr{T}_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> provided <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>. This spine gives a model for <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\PMod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>PMod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\PMod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is of minimal dimension for the mapping class group <math id="S1.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math> of an orientable surface with exactly one puncture; see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>, Theorem 2.1]</cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CJLS24</a>, Introduction]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, Aramayona and Martínez Pérez proved that the mapping class group of any closed orientable surface <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> admits a cocompact classifying space for proper actions of dimension equal to its virtual cohomological dimension. They use their result and the Birman exact sequence to show that is also the case for the <span id="S1.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">pure</span> mapping class group <math id="S1.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\PMod^{n}_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>PMod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\PMod^{n}_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Corollary 1.3]</cite>; see also  arXiv:1302.4238v.2 and
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">CJLS24</a>, Corollary 3.5]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">However, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> does not imply the result for the <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full</span> mapping class group <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> has been misquoted in the literature. For instance, the existence of a model of minimal dimension for the <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full</span> mapping class group is used in the proofs of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">JPTN16</a>, Theorem 1 <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&amp;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;</annotation></semantics></math> Main Theorem]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">NP18</a>, Proposition 5.3 <math id="S1.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&amp;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;</annotation></semantics></math> Theorem 1.4]</cite>, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">AJPTN18</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite>, and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">JRLASS24</a>, Theorem 1.5]</cite>. Our <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> fills this <span id="S1.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">gap</span>, and together with <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Theorem 1.1]</cite> it gives the necessary ingredients to prove the following result.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.2</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Proposition 5.3 of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">NP18</a>]</cite>)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>S</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S</annotation></semantics></math> be a closed (possibly disconnected) surface with possible
a finite number of punctures. Then there is a cocompact model for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod(S)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod(S)</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod(S))=\vcd(\Mod(S))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod(S))=\vcd(\Mod(S))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.2. </span>Proper geometric dimension of spherical braid groups</h3>

<div id="S1.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S1.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">full <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>-th strand spherical braid group <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}(S_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}(S_{0})</annotation></semantics></math></span> is the fundamental group of the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>th unordered configuration space of the sphere <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math>. It is the trivial group for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math> and a cyclic group of order <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>. For all <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> these groups are virtually torsion free and their virtual cohomological dimension is <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\max\{0,n-3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\max\{0,n-3\}</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">GcG17</a>, Theorem 5]</cite>. For <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> the mapping class group <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is related to the <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}(S_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}(S_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> by the following central extension (see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">GJP15</a>, Section 2.4]</cite>)</p>
<table id="S1.Ex2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S1.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}/2\rightarrow B_{n}(S_{0})\rightarrow\Mod(S_{0}^{n})\rightarrow 1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}/2\rightarrow B_{n}(S_{0})\rightarrow\Mod(S_{0}^{n})\rightarrow 1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.SS2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that any cocompact model for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod(S_{0}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod(S_{0}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> is, via the above short exact sequence, a cocompact model for <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}B_{n}(S_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}B_{n}(S_{0})</annotation></semantics></math>. It follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc05</a>, Lemma 5.8]</cite> that <math id="S1.SS2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\gdfin(\Mod(S_{0}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Mod</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em">CLOSE</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\gdfin(\Mod(S_{0}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, and <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> implies:</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_corollary">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Corollary 1.3</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>,
<math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\max\{0,n-3\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">B</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi mathvariant="normal">S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))=\max\{0,n-3\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, there exists a cocompact model for <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}B_{n}(S_{0})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}B_{n}(S_{0})</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S1.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(B_{n}(S_{0}))</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.3. </span>Overview of the proof and the paper</h3>

<div id="S1.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to prove our main result <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we follow the general strategy of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>]</cite>, and obtain the proper geometric dimension <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by computing its algebraic counterpart <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. This computation amounts to verify that for any finite subgroup <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the inequatily <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> holds, and use <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Theorem 3.3]</cite> (see <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> below). Here <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NF</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the <span id="S1.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">normalizer</span> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> is the <span id="S1.SS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">length</span> of <math id="S1.SS3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we introduce the necessary definitions and details.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">An application of Nielsen realization and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> due to Maher, imply that <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)=\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)=\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}})</annotation></semantics></math> for any finite subgroup <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. The parameters <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are invariants of the orbifold quotient <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> as described in <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>. Most of the present paper deals with either computing or upper-bounding the parameters <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once computed <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can use Harer’s computation of the <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>vcd</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd</annotation></semantics></math> for mapping class groups (<a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) to obtain <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}})</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> can be bounded by the number of factors in the prime decomposition of <math id="S1.SS3.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|F|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|F|</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain <a href="#S6.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 6.2. ‣ 6. Genus at least 3 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.2</span></a> as a straightforward application of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Proposition 4.4]</cite>, which we explain in <a href="#S6" title="6. Genus at least 3 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>. However, for genus <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=2</annotation></semantics></math>, an independent and careful analysis of the finite subgroups of <math id="S1.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is required. This is done in Sections <a href="#S3" title="3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, <a href="#S4" title="4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> and <a href="#S5" title="5. Genus 2 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, respectively.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS3.p4" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="S1.SS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Acknowledgments.</span> The authors thank Omar Antolín Camarena and Jesús Nuñez Zimbrón for useful discussions. The first author was funded by CONAHCyT through the program <span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Estancias Posdoctorales por México.</span> The third author’s work was supported by UNAM <span id="S1.SS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Posdoctoral Program (POSDOC)</span>. All authors are grateful for the financial support of DGAPA-UNAM grant PAPIIT IA106923.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2. </span>Preliminaries</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1. </span>Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are several notions of dimension defined for a given group <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall in this section the notions that will be used in this paper.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">A model for the <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">classifying space of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> for proper actions <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math></span> is a <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-CW-complex <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> such that the fixed point set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{H}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mi>H</mi></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{H}</annotation></semantics></math> of a subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H&lt;G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&lt;</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H&lt;G</annotation></semantics></math> is contractible if <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is finite, and is empty otherwise. Such a model always exists and is unique up to proper <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-homotopy. The <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proper geometric dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></span> is defined to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex3" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(G)=\{n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}:\ \text{there exists an $n$-dimensional model for }\underline{E}G\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mtext>there exists an </mtext><mi>n</mi><mtext>-dimensional model for </mtext></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(G)=\{n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}:\ \text{there exists an $n$-dimensional model for }\underline{E}G\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">On the other hand, let <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</span> be the collection of finite subgroups of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, and consider the restricted orbit category <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G</annotation></semantics></math>, which has as objects
the homogeneous <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-spaces <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G/H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G/H</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</span>, and morphisms are <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>-maps. A <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G</annotation></semantics></math>-module is a
contravariant functor from <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc Fin}}G</annotation></semantics></math> to the category of abelian groups, and a morphism between
two <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is a natural transformation of the underlying functors.
The category <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G</annotation></semantics></math>-mod of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G</annotation></semantics></math>-modules is an abelian category with enough
projectives; see for example <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">MV03</a>, p.7]</cite>. The <span id="S2.SS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">proper cohomological dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></span> is defined to be</p>
<table id="S2.Ex4" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(G)=\min\left\{n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}:\ \exists\text{ a projective resolution of the $\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G$-module $\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}$ of length $n$}\right\}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>∈</mo><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub></mrow><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.778em">:</mo><mrow><mo rspace="0.167em">∃</mo><mrow><mtext> a projective resolution of the </mtext><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><mtext>-module </mtext><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mtext> of length </mtext><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(G)=\min\left\{n\in\mathbb{Z}_{\geq 0}:\ \exists\text{ a projective resolution of the $\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G$-module $\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}$ of length $n$}\right\},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}</annotation></semantics></math> is the constant <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G</annotation></semantics></math>-module given by <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}(G/H)=\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ℤ</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}(G/H)=\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in{\text{\sc{Fin}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in{\text{\sc{Fin}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, and every morphism of <math id="S2.SS1.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒪</mi><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">Fin</mtext></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{O}_{\text{\sc{Fin}}}G</annotation></semantics></math> goes to the identity function.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">cohomological dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cd(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>cd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cd(H)</annotation></semantics></math></span> of a group <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> is the length of shortest projective resolution, in the category of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-modules, for the trivial <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>-module <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ℤ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually torsion free, then <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> contains a torsion free subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of finite index, and the <span id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">virtual cohomological dimension of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math></span> is defined to be <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(G)=\cd(H)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>cd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(G)=\cd(H)</annotation></semantics></math>. A well-known theorem of Serre stablishes that <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is well defined and it does not depend on the choice of the finite index torsion free subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. For every virtually torsion free group <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we have the following inequalities (see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLN01</a>, Theorem 2]</cite>)</p>
<table id="S2.E1" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(1)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(G)\leq\cdfin(G)\leq\gdfin(G)\leq\max\{3,\cdfin(G)\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(G)\leq\cdfin(G)\leq\gdfin(G)\leq\max\{3,\cdfin(G)\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">Following the general strategy of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>]</cite>, we will determine the proper geometric dimension <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> by computing the algebraic counterpart <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>. We recall here the two main properties of <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(G)</annotation></semantics></math> that we will need to obtain <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Firstable, the following theorem is a consequence of
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc89</a>, Theorem 13.19]</cite>, as explained in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>, Section 3]</cite>; see also <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">BLN01</a>, Theorem 1]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a group with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(G)=d\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">d</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(G)=d\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>. Then there is a <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>-dimensional <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math>. Morevoer, if <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> has a cocompact model for <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math>, then it also admits a cocompact <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="d" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>d</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">d</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">Now, let <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. We denote by <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{G}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{G}(F)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N_{G}(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N_{G}(F)</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W_{G}(F)=N_{G}(F)/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>W</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>N</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W_{G}(F)=N_{G}(F)/F</annotation></semantics></math> the centralizer, the normalizer, and the <span id="S2.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Weyl group of</span> <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. If there is no risk of confusion we will omit the parenthesis and subindices, i.e. we will use the notation <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NF</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="WF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">WF</annotation></semantics></math>.
The <em id="S2.SS1.p7.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">length</em> <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> of a finite group <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the largest <math id="S2.SS1.p7.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> for which there is a sequence</p>
<table id="S2.Ex5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1=F_{0}&lt;F_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;F_{i}=F." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&lt;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo rspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0.1389em">⋯</mo><mo lspace="0.1389em">&lt;</mo><msub><mi>F</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1=F_{0}&lt;F_{1}&lt;\cdots&lt;F_{i}=F.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p8.1" class="ltx_p">The second useful result to obtain our <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> is the following theorem by Aramayona and Martínez Pérez that gives us a criterion to compute the proper cohomological dimension. It is a consequence from a result of Connolly and Kozniewski <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">CK86</a>, Theorem A]</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 2.2</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a virtually torsion free group such that for any <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq G</annotation></semantics></math> finite subgroup, <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(G)=\vcd(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(G)=\vcd(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 1</span></span><span id="Thmremark1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite subgroup of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> the normalizer <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NF</annotation></semantics></math> and the Weyl group <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="WF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">WF</annotation></semantics></math> are commensurable, therefore <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)=\vcd(WF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)=\vcd(WF)</annotation></semantics></math>. In our analysis below, we will obtain upper bounds of <math id="Thmremark1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)</annotation></semantics></math> in order to apply <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p9.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma will be useful in our computations below. For a given <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in G</annotation></semantics></math>, we denote (respectively) by <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z(g)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="W(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">W(g)</annotation></semantics></math> the centralizer, the normalizer and the Weyl group of the cyclic subgroup <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\langle g\rangle" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\langle g\rangle</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p9.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.3</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> be a virtually torsion free group and let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> a finite subgroup. Then</span></p>
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)\leq\log_{2}(|F|)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)\leq\log_{2}(|F|)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)\leq\min\{\vcd(Z(g))|g\in F\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>min</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)\leq\min\{\vcd(Z(g))|g\in F\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S2.SS1.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.SS1.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Item (1) is <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">HSSTN22</a>, Lemma 3.1]</cite>.
For item (2) note that <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="ZF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF</annotation></semantics></math> is commensurable with <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="WF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">WF</annotation></semantics></math>, hence they have the same <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>vcd</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd</annotation></semantics></math>. The conclusion follows from the fact that <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="ZF=\cap_{g\in F}Z(g)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mi>F</mi><mo rspace="0em">=</mo><msub><mo lspace="0em">∩</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">ZF=\cap_{g\in F}Z(g)</annotation></semantics></math> and the monotonicity of the <math id="S2.SS1.p10.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>vcd</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2. </span>Mapping class groups</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For any <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, the group <math id="S2.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually torsion free and its virtual cohomological dimension was computed by J. L. Harer in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Har86</a>]</cite>:</p>
<table id="S2.E2" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_left"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_left">(2)</span></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.E2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }g=0\text{ and }n&lt;3\\
n-3&amp;\text{if }g=0\text{ and }n\geq 3\\
1&amp;\text{if }g=1\text{ and }n=0\\
4g-5&amp;\text{if }g\geq 2\text{ and }n=0\\
4g-4+n&amp;\text{if }g\geq 1\text{ and }n\geq 1.\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mrow><mtext>if </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mtext> and </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n})=\begin{cases}0&amp;\text{if }g=0\text{ and }n&lt;3\\
n-3&amp;\text{if }g=0\text{ and }n\geq 3\\
1&amp;\text{if }g=1\text{ and }n=0\\
4g-5&amp;\text{if }g\geq 2\text{ and }n=0\\
4g-4+n&amp;\text{if }g\geq 1\text{ and }n\geq 1.\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Nielsen realization (<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">FM12</a>, Theorem 7.2]</cite>,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ker83</a>]</cite>), there is a hyperbolic structure for <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> on which <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> acts by orientation-preserving isometries,
when <math id="S2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\chi(S_{g}^{n})&lt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>χ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&lt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\chi(S_{g}^{n})&lt;0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">By a slight abuse of notation, we denote by <math id="S2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> both the orbifold and the underlying topological surface. In what follows we will use the following notation:</p>
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the genus of <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of elliptic (orbifold) points of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> the number <math id="S2.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="o_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">o_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> of elliptic points plus the number of orbits of punctures of this action</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(g_{F};p_{1}^{F},\ldots,p_{o_{F}}^{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(g_{F};p_{1}^{F},\ldots,p_{o_{F}}^{F})</annotation></semantics></math> is often called the <span id="S2.I2.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">signature of <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math></span>, where <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{1}^{F},\ldots,p_{o_{F}}^{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>F</mi></msubsup><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mi>F</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{1}^{F},\ldots,p_{o_{F}}^{F}</annotation></semantics></math> are the orders of the elliptic points of <math id="S2.I2.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Notice that <math id="S2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}\leq\frac{n}{|F|}+o_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}\leq\frac{n}{|F|}+o_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma is due to Maher. Together with Harer’s computation <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>, it gives us a way to compute the <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>vcd</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd</annotation></semantics></math> of the Weyl group <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="WF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">WF</annotation></semantics></math> of any finite subgroup <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 2.4</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mah11</a><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 2.3]</span></cite><span id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup with <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> as before. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="NF" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">NF</annotation></semantics></math> has finite index in <math id="S2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular,</span></p>
<table id="S2.Ex6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)=\vcd(NF)=\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)=\vcd(NF)=\vcd(\Mod_{g_{F}}^{n_{F}}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3. </span>Genus 0</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The main result of this section is <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> which proves the case <math id="S3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=0</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. In the first half of this section we study the finite subgroups of <math id="S3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and their actions on <math id="S3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, our analysis has as a starting point Stukow’s classification of maximal finite subgroups of <math id="S3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> given in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stu04</a>]</cite>. In the second half of the section we apply the results in the first half to prove <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a>. It is worth saying that we dealt with cases <math id="S3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq n\leq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq n\leq 11</annotation></semantics></math> one by one, and all the computations of <math id="S3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> are summarized in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 for 5≤𝑛≤13 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a> by means of several tables.</p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1. </span>Finite subgroups of <math id="S3.SS1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and their realization</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We start by recalling Stukow’s classification of maximal finite subgroups of <math id="S3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stu04</a><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 3]</span></cite><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
A finite subgroup <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a maximal finite subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> if and only if <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to one of the following:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">a cyclic group </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S3.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\neq 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≠</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\neq 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the dihedral group </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of order </span><math id="S3.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the dihedral group </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2(n-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2(n-2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of order </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the alternating group </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 12)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the symmetric group </span><math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathfrak{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathfrak{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 0,2,6,8,12,14,18" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>18</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 0,2,6,8,12,14,18</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="20\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 24)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 24</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">20\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 24)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">the alternating group </span><math id="S3.I1.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> if </span><math id="S3.I1.i6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\equiv 0,2,12,20,30,32,42" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≡</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>30</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>42</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\equiv 0,2,12,20,30,32,42</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I1.i6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="50\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 12)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>50</mn><mo lspace="0.500em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>mod</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn> 12</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">50\ (\mathrm{mod}\ 12)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I1.i6.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Stukow also studied the conjugacy classes of finite subgroups of <math id="S3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stu04</a><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Theorem 4, Corollary 5]</span></cite><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the set of conjugacy classes of subgroups of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> isomorphic to <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> has two elements if</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I2" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathbb{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathbb{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> even.</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m|n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m|n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m|n-2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">and one element otherwise. In particular, any two maximal finite subgroups of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> are conjugate if and only if they are isomorphic.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Since, for a given finite subgroup <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the number <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on the conjugacy class of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>, we only have to work with a representative of such conjugacy class. Let us describe representatives of the conjugacy classes of maximal finite subgroups of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> of types (1)-(3) following the numeration of <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>. For this let us denote by <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math> the north and south poles of <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. Also given any group isomorphic to <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> recall that there is an index 2 cyclic subgroup of order <math id="S3.SS1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, we call this subgroup the <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">subgroup of rotations</em> and every element which do not belong to the subgroup of rotations is called a <em id="S3.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">reflection</em> (all such elements have order 2).</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I3" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I3.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Up to a self homeomorphism of <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the action of <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as follows: we place one of the punctures on <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> on the equator equidistantly. The generator of <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by a rotation of angle <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2\pi/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>π</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2\pi/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math> that fixes <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I3.i1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Up to a self homeomorphism of <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, the action of <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as follows: we place all the punctures along the equator equidistantly. As in the previous case we have a natural action of the subgroup of rotations <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/n</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, and a reflection can be realized as a half turn that interchanges <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I3.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math> and leave the set of punctures invariant.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I3.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I3.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I3.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The realization of <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2(n-1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2(n-1)}</annotation></semantics></math> is completely analogous to the previous case, the only difference is that we place 2 punctures on <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining <math id="S3.I3.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math> on the equator.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The numeration in the following proposition is designed to be compatible with the numeration in <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. We will use the above descriptions of the actions of maximal finite groups in the proof of the following statement.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then the following holds:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I4" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I4.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I4.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m\leq\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m\leq\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=2+\frac{n-1}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=2+\frac{n-1}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a subgroup of the rotation subgroup </span><math id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=2+\frac{n}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=2+\frac{n}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong D_{2m}\leq D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong D_{2m}\leq D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> odd, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=1+\frac{n+3m}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=1+\frac{n+3m}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> even and </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m|n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=\begin{cases}1+\frac{n+2m}{2m}\\
1+\frac{n+4m}{2m}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=\begin{cases}1+\frac{n+2m}{2m}\\
1+\frac{n+4m}{2m}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">corresponding to the two conjugacy classes of subgroups isomorphic to </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> even and </span><math id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m\not|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m\not|n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix2.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=1+\frac{n+3m}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=1+\frac{n+3m}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> be a subgroup of the rotation subgroup </span><math id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> of </span><math id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2(n-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2(n-2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex12" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=2+\frac{n-2}{m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=2+\frac{n-2}{m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I4.ix4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I4.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong D_{2m}\leq D_{2(n-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong D_{2m}\leq D_{2(n-2)}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m&gt;1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m&gt;1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> odd, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex13" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=1+\frac{n-2+3m}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=1+\frac{n-2+3m}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> even and </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m|(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m|(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex14" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=\begin{cases}1+\frac{n-2+2m}{2m}\\
1+\frac{n-2+4m}{2m}\end{cases}" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>{</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt" displaystyle="true" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mstyle displaystyle="false"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mtd><mtd></mtd></mtr></mtable></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=\begin{cases}1+\frac{n-2+2m}{2m}\\
1+\frac{n-2+4m}{2m}\end{cases}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">corresponding to the two conjugacy classes of subgroups isomorphic to </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span>
<br class="ltx_break"><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">If </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> even and </span><math id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m\not|(n-2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m\not|(n-2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I4.ix4.p1.3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex15" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=1+\frac{n-2+3m}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=1+\frac{n-2+3m}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We proceed by cases:</span></p>
<ol id="S3.I5" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I5.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We can realize the <math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>-action placing a puncture in <math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and the remaining <math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> equidistantly on the equator. Hence {<math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math>} and {<math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\Sur\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur\}</annotation></semantics></math> are both orbits of elliptic points of the <math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>-action, while the orbits of punctures are are exactly <math id="S3.I5.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(n-1)/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(n-1)/m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2.1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The <math id="S3.I5.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>-action can be realized placing all punctures on the equator equidistantly. The conclusion is completely analogous to case (1).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I5.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2.2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I5.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Recall that <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> is generated by the rotation subgroup <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m\leq\mathbb{Z}/n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m\leq\mathbb{Z}/n</annotation></semantics></math> and a half turn <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> that interchanges <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we have the orbit <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\Nte,\Sur\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><mo>,</mo><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\Nte,\Sur\}</annotation></semantics></math>. On the other hand, note that the <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>-action is free away from the poles and the equator. Hence any elliptic point other than <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math> is on the equator. Let <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> be the set of all punctures and all the elliptic points on the equator. Note that, for elements of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math>, the identity element fix all points of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, all nontrivial rotations do not fix points in <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, and each of the <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> reflections fix two points in <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math>, hence by the Burnside lemma, the number of orbits of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> is
<math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{|X|+2m}{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{|X|+2m}{2m}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Therefore, if <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>e</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e</annotation></semantics></math> is the number of elliptic points in <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> we have</p>
<table id="S3.Ex16" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=1+\frac{n+e+2m}{2m}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=1+\frac{n+e+2m}{2m}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.I5.ix3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is odd, then the axis points of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> consists exactly of one puncture <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and its antipode <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="-p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">-p</annotation></semantics></math> which is an elliptic point. Therefore there are <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> elliptic points, that is <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I5.ix3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Assume <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Then the axis of reflections in <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> are obtained by <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>-rotating the axis of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> and taking the bisectors of any two consecutive rotations of the axis of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I5.ix3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> divides <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/m</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Let <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed puncture and let <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed generator of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>. Then between <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tp</annotation></semantics></math> there are <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/m-1</annotation></semantics></math> punctures, that is, an odd number of punctures. If <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> fixes two punctures, then each bisector also fixes two punctures. Therefore there are no elliptic points and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=0</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> fixes two elliptic points, then each bisector also fixes two elliptic points. Therefore <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=2m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I5.ix3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.1" class="ltx_p">If <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2m</annotation></semantics></math> does not divide <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/m</annotation></semantics></math> is odd and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math> is even. Let <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed puncture and let <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> be a fixed generator of <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>. Then between <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="tp" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">tp</annotation></semantics></math> there are <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/m-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/m-1</annotation></semantics></math> punctures, that is, an even number of punctures. Hence, whenever <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> fixes two punctures then each bisector fixes two elliptic points, and whenever <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math> fixes two elliptic points then each bisector fixes two punctures. In both scenarios we conclude <math id="S3.I5.ix3.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="e=m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">e=m</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">Cases (3.1) and (3.2) are analogous to cases (2.1) and (2.2) respectively and are left as an exercise to the reader.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2. </span>Proof of the main theorem for genus 0</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We compute in this subsection, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem8" title="Theorem 3.8. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.8</span></a> below, the geometric dimension for <math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(NF)=\max\{n_{F}-3,0\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(NF)=\max\{n_{F}-3,0\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">By Nielsen realization <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by orientation preserving homeomorphisms.
As a straightforward application of the Riemann-Hurwitz theorem, we have that <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> is homeomorphic to a sphere, that is <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Now the result is a consequence of <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 2.4. ‣ 2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.4</span></a> and Harer’s computation of the virtual cohomological dimension for mapping class groups given in <a href="#S2.E2" title="In 2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Eq.</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.5</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of finite order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S3.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> and</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex17" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(W(g))\leq\frac{n}{r}-1\leq\frac{n}{2}-1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(W(g))\leq\frac{n}{r}-1\leq\frac{n}{2}-1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stu04</a>, Theorem 4, Corollary 5]</cite> the element <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> is contained in a unique (up to isotopy) maximal finite cyclic subgroup of order <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-1</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n-2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n-2</annotation></semantics></math>, which by <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>, is the rotation subgroup of one of the maximal subgroups <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="D_{2(n-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">D_{2(n-2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, up to conjugation, <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>g</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized as a rotation that fixes <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Nte" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">N</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Nte</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Sur" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtext class="ltx_font_smallcaps">S</mtext><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Sur</annotation></semantics></math> and the punctures are on the equator and possible in one or both poles. Therefore <math id="S3.SS2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}\leq 2+n/r" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}\leq 2+n/r</annotation></semantics></math>, and the first inequality in our statement follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. The second inequality is clear.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.6</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\in\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>∈</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\in\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> be an element of order <math id="S3.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="r\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">r\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex18" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(W(g))+\lambda(\langle g\rangle)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(W(g))+\lambda(\langle g\rangle)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> we get</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\vcd(W(g))+\lambda(\langle g\rangle)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">⟨</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy="false">⟩</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\vcd(W(g))+\lambda(\langle g\rangle)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex19.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{r}-1+\log_{2}(r)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>r</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{r}-1+\log_{2}(r)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{r}-1+\log_{2}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>r</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{r}-1+\log_{2}(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S3.Ex21"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.Ex21.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since both <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3</annotation></semantics></math> are both increasing functions of <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>, it is easy to verify that</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex22" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex22.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)\leq n-3" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p5.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p5.2.1" class="ltx_text">for <math id="S3.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.7</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of <math id="S3.Thmtheorem7.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex23" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex23.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.5" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="7\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">7\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>, the conclusion follows from the tables in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 for 5≤𝑛≤13 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>, where <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)</annotation></semantics></math> is computed using <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Proposition 3.3. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a>. The remaining of the proof deals with the case <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 14</annotation></semantics></math>.
By <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 3.2. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a>, up to conjugation, <math id="S3.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is a subgroup of one of six possibilities:</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p7" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I6" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I6.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(1)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq\mathbb{Z}/(n-1)</annotation></semantics></math>. The conclusion follows directly from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> for <math id="S3.I6.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(2)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq D_{2n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq D_{2n}</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is cyclic, the result follows from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem6" title="Proposition 3.6. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.6</span></a> for <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 11" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 11</annotation></semantics></math>. Assume that <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong D_{2m}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong D_{2m}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m|n</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we get</p>
<table id="S3.Ex24" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex24.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)\leq\vcd(\mathbb{Z}/m)\leq\frac{n}{m}-1\quad\text{and}\quad\lambda(F)\leq\log_{2}(m)+1." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mtext>and</mtext><mspace style="width:1em;" width="1em"></mspace><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)\leq\vcd(\mathbb{Z}/m)\leq\frac{n}{m}-1\quad\text{and}\quad\lambda(F)\leq\log_{2}(m)+1.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus</p>
<table id="S3.Ex25" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex25.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{m}+\log_{2}(m)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mi>m</mi></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{m}+\log_{2}(m)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I6.i2.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Now note that <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.I6.i2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 14" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 14</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq D_{2(n-2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq D_{2(n-2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. This case is analogous to the previous one.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. We have <math id="S3.I6.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)\leq\lambda(A_{4})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)\leq\lambda(A_{4})=3</annotation></semantics></math>. By <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem5" title="Proposition 3.5. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.5</span></a> we get</p>
<table id="S3.Ex26" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex26.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+3." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+3.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.I6.i4.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Note that <math id="S3.I6.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{2}+3\leq n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{2}+3\leq n-3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.I6.i4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 10</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq\mathfrak{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq\mathfrak{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogous to case (4).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I6.i6" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(6)</span> 
<div id="S3.I6.i6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I6.i6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S3.I6.i6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\leq A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\leq A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math>. Analogous to case (4).</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 3.8</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S3.Thmtheorem8.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex27" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex27.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\max\{n-3,0\}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>max</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\max\{n-3,0\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.6" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS2.p9" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p9.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p9.1.1" class="ltx_text">We divide the proof in cases.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p10" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p10.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p10.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=0,1,2,3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=0,1,2,3</annotation></semantics></math>. In these cases <math id="S3.SS2.p10.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> is a finite group and the conclusion follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p11" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p11.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p11.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=4</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{4})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{4})=1</annotation></semantics></math>, we know that <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually finitely generated free, hence by a well-known theorem of Stallings, <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> acts properly on a tree and this action provides a model for <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{0}^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{0}^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension 1, therefore <math id="S3.SS2.p11.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{4})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{4})=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>4</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{4})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{4})=1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p12" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case, by Birman-Hilden theory we have a central extension</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex28" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex28.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to\mathbb{Z}/2\to\Mod_{2}\to\Mod_{0}^{6}\to 1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to\mathbb{Z}/2\to\Mod_{2}\to\Mod_{0}^{6}\to 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p12.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p12.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the kernel is generated by the hyperelliptic involution of <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. By <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ji14</a>, Proposition 4.3]</cite> there is a model <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension 3. Therefore <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X^{\mathbb{Z}/2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X^{\mathbb{Z}/2}</annotation></semantics></math> is a model for <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{0}^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{0}^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> of dimension at most 3. We conclude that <math id="S3.SS2.p12.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{6})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{6})=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p13" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p13.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 7" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 7</annotation></semantics></math>. From <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem7" title="Theorem 3.7. ‣ 3.2. Proof of the main theorem for genus 0 ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.7</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> we obtain the proper cohomological dimension. By the Eilenberg-Ganea theorem, we have that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{0}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>, except possibly when <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=5</annotation></semantics></math>. To rule out that possibility, we consider the following central extension that arises from Birman-Hilden theory</span></p>
<table id="S3.Ex29" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S3.Ex29.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}/2\rightarrow\Mod_{1}^{2}\rightarrow\Mod_{0}^{5}\rightarrow 1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\rightarrow\mathbb{Z}/2\rightarrow\Mod_{1}^{2}\rightarrow\Mod_{0}^{5}\rightarrow 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.SS2.p13.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p13.2.1" class="ltx_text">where the kernel is generated by the hyperelliptic involution of <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. If follows from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc05</a>, Lemma 5.8]</cite> that <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{5})=\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{5})=\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{5})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>5</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{0}^{5})=2</annotation></semantics></math> follows from our computation <math id="S3.SS2.p13.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=2</annotation></semantics></math> in the proof of <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4. </span>Genus 1</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we prove <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Theorem 4.3. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a> which gives the case <math id="S4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g=1</annotation></semantics></math> of <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 1.1. ‣ 1. Introduction ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>. First we consider finite subgroups of <math id="S4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and their actions on <math id="S4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, and let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a finite subgroup of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math> where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> are positive integers such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="st|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">st|n</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1,2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1,2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. Moreover, the quotient <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math> only depends on <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>m</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m</annotation></semantics></math>, and we have the following descriptions of these orbifold quotients:</span></p>
<ul id="S4.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a torus with no elliptic points,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=2</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a sphere with four elliptic points of orders </span><math id="S4.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,2,2,2)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,2,2,2)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a sphere with three elliptic points of orders </span><math id="S4.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(3,3,3)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(3,3,3)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=4</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a sphere with three elliptic points of orders </span><math id="S4.I1.i4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,4,4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,4,4)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i4.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, and</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I1.i5" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I1.i5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">for </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=6</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> is a sphere with three elliptic points of orders </span><math id="S4.I1.i5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(2,3,6)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(2,3,6)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S4.I1.i5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be as in the statement, by capping the punctures, the action of <math id="S4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> leads to an action of <math id="S4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> on the torus <math id="S4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. By the uniformization theorem, there is a metric of constant curvature 0 on <math id="S4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> such that the action of <math id="S4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is by isometries. Next we can lift this action to the universal covering, that is, there is a group <math id="S4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> acting by orientation preserving euclidean isometries on <math id="S4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that is a central extension of <math id="S4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> by a rank <math id="S4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> group of translations, and such that <math id="S4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}/\widetilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}/\widetilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S4.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence <math id="S4.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is a wallpaper group without reflections nor glide reflections, and therefore <math id="S4.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\widetilde{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>F</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\widetilde{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is isomorphic to <math id="S4.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S4.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=1,2,3,4," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=1,2,3,4,</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that there are positive integers <math id="S4.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="s" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>s</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">s</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong(\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m)/(s\mathbb{Z}\oplus t\mathbb{Z})\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>⊕</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong(\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m)/(s\mathbb{Z}\oplus t\mathbb{Z})\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math>. Furthermore, note that <math id="S4.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t</annotation></semantics></math> acts freely on <math id="S4.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and preserves the set of punctures in <math id="S4.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which is a set with <math id="S4.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> elements, as a conclusion <math id="S4.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="st|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">st|n</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the </span><span id="S4.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">moreover part</span><span id="S4.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text"> of the statement, observe that <math id="S4.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S4.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{R}^{2}/(\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>ℝ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>/</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msup><mi>ℤ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{R}^{2}/(\mathbb{Z}^{2}\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m)</annotation></semantics></math>, and the latter quotients are well known, see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thu22</a>, Theorem 13.3.6]</cite>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every finite subgroup <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex30" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex30.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">The conclusion is clear when <math id="S4.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> is the trivial subgroup.
Let <math id="S4.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a nontrivial finite subgroup of <math id="S4.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, then by Nielsen realization theorem we can realize <math id="S4.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> as a finite group of orientation-preserving diffeomorphisms of <math id="S4.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. We proceed by cases following the notation and conclusions in <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p5" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S4.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S4.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="st|n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">st|n</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we have <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=n/st" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=n/st</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/st" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/st</annotation></semantics></math> punctures. Hence by <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 2.3. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Lemma</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.3</span></a>(1) we get</p>
<table id="S4.Ex31" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex31.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n_{F}})+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{st}+\log_{2}(n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n_{F}})+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{st}+\log_{2}(n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now notice that <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.I2.i1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we have <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to a sphere with 4 elliptic points. Hence we conclude that <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}\leq 4+n/st\leq 4+n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}\leq 4+n/st\leq 4+n/2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)=n_{F}-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)=n_{F}-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</p>
<table id="S4.Ex32" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex32.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+1+\log_{2}(2n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+2+\log_{2}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+1+\log_{2}(2n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+2+\log_{2}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now notice that <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2+2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2+2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.I2.i2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S4.I2.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S4.I2.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋊</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong(\mathbb{Z}/s\times\mathbb{Z}/t)\rtimes\mathbb{Z}/m</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m=3,4,6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m=3,4,6</annotation></semantics></math>. In this case we have <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{1}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{1}/F</annotation></semantics></math> is diffeomorphic to a sphere with 3 elliptic points. Hence we conclude that <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}\leq 4+n/st\leq 3+n/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mi>s</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}\leq 4+n/st\leq 3+n/2</annotation></semantics></math>, and <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)=n_{F}-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)=n_{F}-3</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</p>
<table id="S4.Ex33" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex33.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(6n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+2+\log_{2}(n)." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\frac{n}{2}+\log_{2}(6n)\leq\frac{n}{2}+2+\log_{2}(n).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.I2.i3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Now notice that <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n/2+2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n/2+2+\log_{2}(n)\leq n</annotation></semantics></math> for all <math id="S4.I2.i3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S4.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">To finish the proof we only have to verify the statement for <math id="S4.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2,3,4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2,3,4</annotation></semantics></math>. From <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 4.1. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> we can describe explicitly the finite subgroups of <math id="S4.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and their quotients, and the analysis can be done case by case; the details are left to the reader.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>. Then</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex34" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex34.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n}))=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo fence="true" rspace="0em">OPEN</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n}))=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.4" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S4.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p7.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p7.1.1" class="ltx_text">For <math id="S4.p7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, the result follows since <math id="S4.p7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}^{1}\cong\text{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mtext>SL</mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mi>ℤ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}^{1}\cong\text{SL}(2,\mathbb{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually free.
For <math id="S4.p7.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 2</annotation></semantics></math>, <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 4.2. ‣ 4. Genus 1 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> imply <math id="S4.p7.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.p8" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p8.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.1.1" class="ltx_text">By the Eilenberg-Ganea theorem, we have that <math id="S4.p8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{n})=n</annotation></semantics></math>, except possibly for <math id="S4.p8.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=2</annotation></semantics></math>. Let us rule out this possibility. We have the following short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S4.Ex35" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.Ex35.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to B_{2}(S_{1})/Z\to\Mod_{1}^{2}\to\Mod_{1}\to 1," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to B_{2}(S_{1})/Z\to\Mod_{1}^{2}\to\Mod_{1}\to 1,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.p8.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.p8.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S4.p8.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(S_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(S_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> is the braid group over the torus on 2 strands, and <math id="S4.p8.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Z</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z</annotation></semantics></math> is its center. On the other hand <math id="S4.p8.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{2}(S_{1})/Z\cong\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>/</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo>≅</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{2}(S_{1})/Z\cong\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math>,
see for instance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">BG05</a>, Proposition 4.4(i)]</cite>. Note that <math id="S4.p8.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ℤ</mi><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∗</mo><mi>ℤ</mi></mrow><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2*\mathbb{Z}/2</annotation></semantics></math> is virtually finitely generated free, hence every finite extension <math id="S4.p8.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> of this group admits a proper action on a tree, which provides a 1-dimensional model for <math id="S4.p8.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}G</annotation></semantics></math>. The group <math id="S4.p8.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> also admits a 1-dimensional model for <math id="S4.p8.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>, hence by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lüc05</a>, Theorem 5.16]</cite>, there is a 2-dimensional model for <math id="S4.p8.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\underline{E}\Mod_{1}^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\underline{E}\Mod_{1}^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. This establishes that <math id="S4.p8.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=\cdfin(\Mod_{1}^{2})=2</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5. </span>Genus 2</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In order to compute the proper geometric dimension of <math id="S5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, with <math id="S5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, we use Broughton’s complete classification, up to topological equivalence, of finite group actions on a genus <math id="S5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> surface <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro91</a>, Theorem 4.1 <math id="S5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\&amp;" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo>&amp;</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\&amp;</annotation></semantics></math> Table 4]</cite>, see <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_2^𝑛. ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Appendix</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>. Notice that there are only finitely many conjugacy classes of finite groups that act on <math id="S5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> by homeomorphism. Hence by Nielsen realization theorem, given <math id="S5.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 0</annotation></semantics></math>, any finite subgroup <math id="S5.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> can be realized, up to conjugation, by one of these finite possibilities. This makes the genus 2 case different in nature to the cases of genus 0 and 1 where the isomorphism types of finite subgroups of the corresponding mapping class groups depend strongly on <math id="S5.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 5.1</span></span><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. Then for every non-trivial finite subgroup <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S5.Ex36" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S5.Ex36.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular <math id="S5.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\gdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\gdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S5.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S5.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Since <math id="S5.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})\geq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})\geq 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})\geq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})\geq 5</annotation></semantics></math>, we have <math id="S5.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\gdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\cdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})=\gdfin(\Mod_{2}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math> for every <math id="S5.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S5.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be a non-trivial finite subgroup of <math id="S5.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. By Nielsen realization <math id="S5.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> acts on <math id="S5.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> by orientation preserving homeomorphisms. Recall from <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2. Mapping class groups ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Section</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> that <math id="S5.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is bounded above by <math id="S5.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\frac{n}{|F|}+o_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\frac{n}{|F|}+o_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">According to Broughton’s classification the quotient <math id="S5.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S^{n}_{2}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S^{n}_{2}/F</annotation></semantics></math> can only have genus <math id="S5.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S5.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}=1</annotation></semantics></math>. When <math id="S5.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}=0</annotation></semantics></math>, we have that <math id="S5.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)=\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n_{F}})=n_{F}-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)=\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n_{F}})=n_{F}-3</annotation></semantics></math>
and when <math id="S5.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g_{F}=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>g</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g_{F}=1</annotation></semantics></math>, then <math id="S5.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n_{F}})=n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)=\vcd(\Mod_{1}^{n_{F}})=n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. In <a href="#A2.T11" title="In Appendix B 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_2^𝑛. ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> we recall the classification from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bro91</a>, Table 4]</cite>, and we give explicit upper bounds for <math id="S5.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S5.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)</annotation></semantics></math>. From this we can see that the inequality <math id="S5.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2}^{n})=n+4</annotation></semantics></math> holds for almost all finite subgroups <math id="S5.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S5.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>. The only exception is the case of <math id="S5.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\text{GL}_{2}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>GL</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\text{GL}_{2}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S5.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=1</annotation></semantics></math>, which does not occur since <math id="S5.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=\text{GL}_{2}(4)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mtext>GL</mtext><mn>2</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=\text{GL}_{2}(4)</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be realized as subgroup of <math id="S5.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}^{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>1</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}^{1}</annotation></semantics></math>; indeed since the only puncture will be a fixed point of the action, but as we can read in the signature of this action in <a href="#A2.T11" title="In Appendix B 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_2^𝑛. ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> there are no fixed points.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="Thmremark2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="Thmremark2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Remark 2</span></span><span id="Thmremark2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="Thmremark2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="Thmremark2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">From <a href="#A2.T11" title="In Appendix B 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_2^𝑛. ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> we can see that there are several finite subgroups <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> such that
<math id="Thmremark2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\not\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≰</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\not\leq\vcd(\Mod_{2})=3</annotation></semantics></math>. For instance, <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)=4</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F=D_{2(6)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>D</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F=D_{2(6)}</annotation></semantics></math>. Hence, we cannot use this strategy to obtain <math id="Thmremark2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\Mod_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>Mod</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\Mod_{2})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6. </span>Genus at least 3</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section we promote the results in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a>]</cite> for <math id="S6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, to <math id="S6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S6.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math>, using a simple argument.</p>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 6.1</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">[</span><a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">AMP14</a><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, Proposition 4.4]</span></cite><span id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">
For any <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math> and any finite subgroup <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex37" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex37.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 6.2</span></span><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">For any <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="g\geq 3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">g\geq 3</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n\geq 1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n\geq 1</annotation></semantics></math> and any finite subgroup <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> we have</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex38" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex38.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n}).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">In particular <math id="S6.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gdfin(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})=\cdfin(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})=\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>gd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><munder accentunder="true"><mi>cd</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">g</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gdfin(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})=\cdfin(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})=\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{n})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S6.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S6.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let us consider the Birman short exact sequence <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bir74</a>, Theorem 4.3]</cite></span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex39" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex39.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to B_{n}(S_{g})\to\Mod_{g}^{n}\xrightarrow{\varphi}\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0}\to 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜑</mo></mover><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to B_{n}(S_{g})\to\Mod_{g}^{n}\xrightarrow{\varphi}\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0}\to 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="S6.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B_{n}(S_{g})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B_{n}(S_{g})</annotation></semantics></math> is the full braid group over <math id="S6.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{g}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{g}</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S6.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>n</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n</annotation></semantics></math> strings.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S6.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">Let <math id="S6.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> be any finite subgroup of <math id="S6.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{g}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{g}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then by restriction of the above sequence we obtain the following short exact sequence</span></p>
<table id="S6.Ex40" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S6.Ex40.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="1\to B_{n}(S_{g})\cap NF\to NF\xrightarrow{\varphi}\varphi(NF)\to 1" display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mover accent="true"><mo>→</mo><mo mathsize="0.700em">𝜑</mo></mover><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">1\to B_{n}(S_{g})\cap NF\to NF\xrightarrow{\varphi}\varphi(NF)\to 1</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.2.1" class="ltx_text">and note that <math id="S6.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\varphi(NF)\leq N\varphi(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>φ</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\varphi(NF)\leq N\varphi(F)</annotation></semantics></math>. Therefore</span></p>
<table id="S6.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S6.Ex41"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex41.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\vcd(NF)+\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\vcd(NF)+\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex41.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\vcd(\varphi(NF))+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g})\cap NF)+\lambda(F),\text{ by subaditivity of $\vcd$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∩</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> by subaditivity of </mtext><mi>vcd</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\vcd(\varphi(NF))+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g})\cap NF)+\lambda(F),\text{ by subaditivity of $\vcd$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex42"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex42.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\vcd(N(\varphi(F)))+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g}))+\lambda(\varphi(F)),\text{ by monotonicity of $\vcd$}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>φ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> by monotonicity of </mtext><mi>vcd</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\vcd(N(\varphi(F)))+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g}))+\lambda(\varphi(F)),\text{ by monotonicity of $\vcd$}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex43"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex43.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0})+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g})),\text{ by \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{AMP:g:atleast:three}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mn>0</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>g</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext> by </mtext><mtext><a href="#S6.Thmtheorem1" title="Proposition 6.1. ‣ 6. Genus at least 3 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Proposition</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6.1</span></a></mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\vcd(\mathrm{Mod}_{g}^{0})+\vcd(B_{n}(S_{g})),\text{ by \lx@cref{creftype~refnum}{AMP:g:atleast:three}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="S6.Ex44"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S6.Ex44.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq 4g-5+n+1=4g+n-4=\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n}),\text{by \cite[cite]{[\@@bibref{}{MR3869010}{}{}, Theorem 1.2]}.}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>g</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mi>g</mi><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mtext>by </mtext><mtext class="ltx_citemacro_cite"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">LGGM18</a>, Theorem 1.2]</cite></mtext><mtext>.</mtext></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq 4g-5+n+1=4g+n-4=\vcd(\Mod_{g}^{n}),\text{by \cite[cite]{[\@@bibref{}{MR3869010}{}{}, Theorem 1.2]}.}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S6.p3.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.p3.3.1" class="ltx_text">Now the result follows. The </span><span id="S6.p3.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">in particular</span><span id="S6.p3.3.3" class="ltx_text"> part follows directly from <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2.2. ‣ 2.1. Proper geometric and cohomological dimensions ‣ 2. Preliminaries ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span><math id="A1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)</annotation></semantics></math> for finite subgroups of <math id="A1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math></h2>

<div id="A1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this appendix we describe <math id="A1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\lambda(F)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\lambda(F)</annotation></semantics></math> for all the conjugacy classes of finite subgroups <math id="A1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> when <math id="A1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5\leq n\leq 13" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>≤</mo><mn>13</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5\leq n\leq 13</annotation></semantics></math>.
<br class="ltx_break"></p>
</div>
<div id="A1.p2" class="ltx_para ltx_noindent">
<p id="A1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Polyhedral subgroups of <math id="A1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span> We keep the numeration from <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Theorem 3.1. ‣ 3.1. Finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 and their realization ‣ 3. Genus 0 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Theorem</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> to analyze the polyhedral subgroups of <math id="A1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6,8,10,12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6,8,10,12</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(3)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math>, up to conjugacy, there is a maximal finite subgroup <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong A_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong A_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be realized as the symmetry group of a tetrahedron. There are three orbits of points with nontrivial stabiliser in <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>: the centers of faces, the centers of edges and the vertices with orbits of length <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Notice that for <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> there must be two orbits of punctures (one of length <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math> and another of length <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>) and one orbit of elliptics, hence <math id="A1.I1.ix1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(4)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6,8,12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6,8,12</annotation></semantics></math>, up to conjugacy, there is a maximal finite subgroup <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong\mathfrak{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong\mathfrak{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be realized as the symmetry group of a cube (octahedron). There are three orbits of points with nontrivial stabiliser in <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>: the centers of faces, the centers of edges and the vertices with stabilisers of order <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>4</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">4</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>2</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">2</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>3</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3</annotation></semantics></math>, respectively. Notice that for <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math> we must place the punctures in the centers of the faces (unique orbit of length <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>6</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">6</annotation></semantics></math>), for <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math> we must place the punctures in the vertices of the cube (unique orbit of length <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>8</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">8</annotation></semantics></math>) and for <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math> the punctures can only be placed in the centers of the edges (unique orbit of length <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence, in <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> there is only one orbit of punctures and two orbits of elliptics and <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.I1.ix2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Now let <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong A_{4}\leq\mathfrak{S}_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>≤</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong A_{4}\leq\mathfrak{S}_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. It can be realized as subgroup of the symmetry group of a tetrahedron embedded into a cube. For <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6</annotation></semantics></math>, there are two orbits of vertices, one orbit of centers of faces (where we have placed the punctures) and the centers of edges are no longer elliptics, hence <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=3</annotation></semantics></math>. For <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8</annotation></semantics></math>, there are two orbits of vertices (where we have placed the punctures), one orbit of centers of faces and the centers of edges are no longer elliptics, hence <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=3</annotation></semantics></math>. Finally, for <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=10" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=10</annotation></semantics></math> there are two orbits of vertices, one orbit of centers of faces and one orbit of the centers of edges (where we have placed the punctures), , hence <math id="A1.I1.ix2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=4" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=4</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(5)</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">For <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math>, up to conjugacy, there is a maximal finite subgroup <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F\cong A_{5}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>≅</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>5</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F\cong A_{5}</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be realized as the symmetry group of a dodecahedron (icosahedron). There are three orbits of points with nontrivial stabiliser in <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>F</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">F</annotation></semantics></math>: the centers of faces, the centers of edges and the vertices. For <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=12</annotation></semantics></math> we must place the punctures in the centers of the faces (unique orbit of length <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>12</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">12</annotation></semantics></math>). Hence, in <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{0}^{n}/F" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo>/</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{0}^{n}/F</annotation></semantics></math> there is only one orbit of punctures and two orbits of elliptics and <math id="A1.I1.ix3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n_{F}=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>n</mi><mi>F</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n_{F}=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We summarize the conclusions in <a href="#A1.T1" title="In Appendix A 𝜆(𝐹) and vcd(𝑊⁢𝐹) for finite subgroups of Mod_0^𝑛 for 5≤𝑛≤13 ‣ The proper geometric dimension of the mapping class group of an orientable surface with punctures" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">Table</span> <span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Notice that <math id="A1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>F</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(WF)+\lambda(F)\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{n})=n-3</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=8,10,12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=8,10,12</annotation></semantics></math>. However, <math id="A1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\vcd(W\mathfrak{S}_{4})+\lambda(\mathfrak{S}_{4})=4\not\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{6})=3" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>λ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝔖</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>≰</mo><mrow><mi>vcd</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\vcd(W\mathfrak{S}_{4})+\lambda(\mathfrak{S}_{4})=4\not\leq\vcd(\Mod_{0}^{6})=3</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="A1.T1" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="A1.T1.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 1</span>. </span><span id="A1.T1.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Polyhedral subgroups of <math id="A1.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Mod_{0}^{n}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi mathsize="1.110em">Mod</mi><mn>0</mn><mi>n</mi></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Mod_{0}^{n}</annotation></semantics></math>, for <math id="A1.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="n=6,8,10,12" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n=6,8,10,12</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></figcaption>
